# SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-4-Clause OR AGPL-3.0-or-later
# formal/formal.build — the formal-methods gate graph (alloy, tla, saw, cbmc,
# k, idris), scheduled by build-unit in the ds-check image, which carries every
# formal toolchain and the DS build toolchain. Each lane is a content-addressed
# stamp step running a thin `python3 scripts/formal/check_<tool>.py` recipe;
# with a warm cache, a passing lane whose inputs are unchanged is reused. Gate
# targets:
#
#   build formal/formal.build alloy.stamp          # one lane
#   build formal/formal.build formal-all.stamp     # every lane
#
# A separate graph from the CI graph: these lanes need no libds or pyds
# runtime; they prove the spec, not the engine.
#
# Public: this file and scripts/formal/ are in the public tree, and nothing
# here reads the private tree. The souffle lane's recipe imports
# scripts/private/formal/, so it runs from its own graph, souffle.build.
#
# In formal/, beside what it builds, and run from the repository root:
# build-unit resolves a graph's paths against the root it is invoked with, so
# every path below is repo-relative.
#
# ── every lane requires `amd64 linux` ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# No formal methods on arm. Without `require`, a step runs wherever build-unit
# runs, and on arm64 the lanes whose tools are arch-neutral (Alloy, TLC, CBMC,
# Idris) would pass beside K's and SAW's refusals and fold into a green
# `formal-all.stamp`. `require` is resolved against build.ini's `[caps]`, so no
# arm64 target is chosen. `scripts/formal/formal_arch.py` is the other half,
# for a recipe run by hand (`python3 scripts/formal/check_alloy.py
# formal/alloy`).
#
# `formal-all.stamp` carries no require: it runs `true` and only folds its
# inputs' digests.
#
# ── what each lane's key covers ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every model, program and expectation file is an input, per file, through the
# globs in each lane's `reads`, so editing one re-runs its lane. The solver is
# an input too, so upgrading Alloy, TLC, SAW, K, clang or the JDK re-keys the
# lanes that use it. The tool paths are params: the graph sets the recipe's
# environment (`env`) from the same value it hashes (`reads`).
#
# The key also covers the C under proof (saw and cbmc prove libds source,
# reached through the shims' `#include`s and the harnesses' `@cbmc-src`), the
# harness that decides (`RunAlloy.java` calls a command passed or failed;
# `formal_arch.py` refuses a machine), and the idris modules, package and test
# pairs.
#
# ── a lane whose corpus vanished fails ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# build-unit fails the build on a glob that matched no files, so every corpus
# glob below is a presence check: no lane runs its solver over an empty
# directory and stamps a green proof of nothing. It catches zero, not wrong:
# deciding whether a model is the right model is what the lane is for. A
# private test proves it fires.

param alloy_jar = /opt/alloy/alloy.jar
param tla_jar   = /opt/tla/tla2tools.jar
param saw_bin   = saw
param saw_clang = clang
param cbmc_bin  = cbmc
# Each CBMC process's address-space cap, in MiB (check_cbmc.py, THE MEMORY
# CAP). It excludes every proof whose model needs more: such an entry point
# fails, never passes.
param cbmc_mem_mib = 3072

# ── alloy: solve formal/alloy/*.als (structure) — every `check` UNSAT, `run` SAT.
#    The graph sets ALLOY_JAR, which check_alloy.py reads, and hashes the same
#    value. javac compiles the RunAlloy harness and java runs it, so a JDK swap
#    changes the key.
step alloy.stamp
  stamp
  require amd64 linux
  run     python3 scripts/formal/check_alloy.py formal/alloy
  env     ALLOY_JAR={alloy_jar}
  reads   scripts/formal/check_alloy.py scripts/formal/formal_arch.py {alloy_jar} javac java
          formal/alloy/RunAlloy.java formal/alloy/*.als

# ── tla: model-check formal/tla/*.tla (behavior) — invariants + temporal props hold.
step tla.stamp
  stamp
  require amd64 linux
  run     python3 scripts/formal/check_tla.py formal/tla
  env     TLA_JAR={tla_jar}
  reads   scripts/formal/check_tla.py scripts/formal/formal_arch.py {tla_jar} java
          formal/tla/*.tla formal/tla/*.cfg

# ── saw: prove formal/saw/*.saw (C ⊨ spec, bit/pointer-exact). clang compiles
#    the C harness to the bitcode SAW proves against, so a clang change changes
#    what was proved.
#
#    `int_ops.h` and `oa.h` are the C under proof. Every function this lane
#    proves is a `static inline` in one of the two, which is why each proof has
#    a shim: the shim gives the function a symbol and holds none of its body.
#    `oa.h` includes `libds.h`, so that is an input by the same route.
step saw.stamp
  stamp
  require amd64 linux
  run     python3 scripts/formal/check_saw.py formal/saw
  env     SAW_BIN={saw_bin}
  env     DS_SAW_CLANG={saw_clang}
  reads   scripts/formal/check_saw.py scripts/formal/formal_arch.py {saw_bin} {saw_clang}
          runtime/src/libds/int_ops.h runtime/src/libds/oa.h runtime/include/libds.h
          formal/saw/*.saw formal/saw/*.c

# ── cbmc: bounded model-checking of libds C against named clause properties.
#    The second `C |= spec` lane, beside saw, and weaker: saw proves
#    equivalence to a Cryptol reference over a whole domain, cbmc proves a
#    stated property up to an unwind bound. The harnesses carry their own
#    bounds in `@cbmc` directives so the claim travels with the proof — the .c
#    is the proof, so one glob covers the corpus.
#
#    The lane's one harness is ldet_3_2_8__fixed.c: float_fmt.c's fixed-
#    precision primitives held to the contracts formal/idris/FixedRound.idr
#    takes them at. Reads the translation units its `@cbmc-src` names, which
#    CBMC is handed beside the harness, and the headers they include.
#
#    Two steps over one corpus. A harness may put entry points after a
#    `@cbmc-step <name>` marker, and `check_cbmc.py --step <name>` runs those
#    alone. `cbmc.stamp` is the default step: every entry point before a
#    marker, which is every one a clause ties. `cbmc-sweep.stamp` is
#    ldet_3_2_8__fixed.c's untied precision sweep (plan_rounds_p2, p3, p6, p10,
#    p18, plan_short_fraction), over two hours on its own, so it is not folded
#    into formal-all.stamp; it runs when named (the test driver's `cbmc-sweep`
#    check). Same inputs.
step cbmc.stamp
  stamp
  require amd64 linux
  run     python3 scripts/formal/check_cbmc.py formal/cbmc
  env     CBMC_BIN={cbmc_bin}
  env     CBMC_MEM_MIB={cbmc_mem_mib}
  reads   scripts/formal/check_cbmc.py scripts/formal/formal_arch.py {cbmc_bin}
          runtime/src/libds/float_fmt.c runtime/src/libds/float_fmt.h
          runtime/src/libds/ds_text.c runtime/src/libds/ds_text.h
          formal/cbmc/*.c

step cbmc-sweep.stamp
  stamp
  require amd64 linux
  run     python3 scripts/formal/check_cbmc.py --step sweep formal/cbmc
  env     CBMC_BIN={cbmc_bin}
  env     CBMC_MEM_MIB={cbmc_mem_mib}
  reads   scripts/formal/check_cbmc.py scripts/formal/formal_arch.py {cbmc_bin}
          runtime/src/libds/float_fmt.c runtime/src/libds/float_fmt.h
          runtime/src/libds/ds_text.c runtime/src/libds/ds_text.h
          formal/cbmc/*.c

# ── k: run formal/k/*.k executable DS operational semantics (language ↦ meaning).
#    No params for kompile/krun: check_k.py takes them off $PATH with no
#    override. Read by name, resolved through $PATH like any other tool, and
#    hashed. .prog and .expected are the lane's cases, and an expectation is as
#    much an input as the model.
step k.stamp
  stamp
  require amd64 linux
  run     python3 scripts/formal/check_k.py formal/k
  reads   scripts/formal/check_k.py scripts/formal/formal_arch.py kompile krun
          formal/k/*.k formal/k/*.prog formal/k/*.expected

# ── idris: typecheck formal/idris/**/*.idr — the ITP lane. The only lane with a
#    totality checker: no other tool here can say that a function terminates
#    on every input, or that a proof rests on no hidden hole. idris2 is read by
#    name, as kompile and krun are.
#
#    The modules are globbed one directory level at a time, as `*.idr` and
#    `*/*.idr`, and not as `**`: the recipe writes `build/` inside the corpus
#    directory, and a per-directory pattern cannot reach into it whatever the
#    walk prunes. A module two levels down is in neither pattern. The package
#    decides what is built and run; the
#    test pairs are the runner's cases, inputs on check_k's reasoning about
#    `.expected`.
step idris.stamp
  stamp
  require amd64 linux
  run     python3 scripts/formal/check_idris.py formal/idris
  reads   scripts/formal/check_idris.py scripts/formal/formal_arch.py idris2
          formal/idris/*.idr formal/idris/*/*.idr formal/idris/*.ipkg
          formal/idris/tests/*.lowered formal/idris/tests/*.expected

# ── instances: one instance drawn in two lanes is one instance.
#    Not a formal method and it proves nothing about the clause. tms_cascade.tla
#    draws the ranked instance and the unfounded cycle (`iret_12__GRAPH`), and
#    Rete/Refcount.idr writes both again as literals (`iret_12_3__tla_js`,
#    `llog_2_5__witness_js`) that its witnesses execute; the [IRET.12.2] tie on
#    `iret_12_2__no_unsupported_forever` cites their agreement. The recipe
#    reads the module's three definitions under each graph, the literals and
#    the signatures that apply the two fixpoints to them, and compares the two
#    under the renaming it declares (the Logician's reading: f1..f4 -> 1..4, P,
#    Q, R -> 0, 1, 2). A mismatch fails it; a definition it cannot find
#    refuses, never passes.
#
#    Keyed on both models and the .cfg that says which graph the ELSE branch is,
#    so an edit to either side re-runs it; the recipe imports check_tla.py's
#    reader, so that is an input too. The tla and idris lanes each prove their
#    own side; this holds that they were run on one instance.
step instances.stamp
  stamp
  require amd64 linux
  run     python3 scripts/formal/check_instances.py formal/tla/tms_cascade.tla formal/tla/tms_cascade.cfg formal/idris/Rete/Refcount.idr
  reads   scripts/formal/check_instances.py scripts/formal/check_tla.py scripts/formal/formal_arch.py
          formal/tla/tms_cascade.tla formal/tla/tms_cascade.cfg formal/idris/Rete/Refcount.idr

# ── formal-all: the aggregate — a stamp step that reads every lane's stamp, so
#    `build formal/formal.build formal-all.stamp` runs (or reuses) every lane,
#    and the instance check beside them. Not cbmc-sweep.stamp, for its cost
#    (the cbmc comment above).
step formal-all.stamp
  stamp
  run   true
  reads alloy.stamp tla.stamp saw.stamp cbmc.stamp k.stamp idris.stamp
        instances.stamp

# ── every lane reads deps.lock ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These lanes need no libds or pyds of their own, but build-unit, which
# schedules them, is a DS program, so the bootstrap installs pyds before this
# graph runs. pyds links libds's `ds_re2.o`, and which RE2 that is comes from
# the dependency lock by way of ds-check's image build. So the lock is an
# input to every lane: a tree whose lock differs mints different step keys,
# and no lane reuses a stamp proved against a different RE2, which is the DS
# regex language under [LREG.7.1].
#
# This prevents false reuse; it does not detect two locks that disagree. The
# image records the lock it was built from, and
# scripts/private/gate/bootstrap.py refuses a tree whose lock differs. A green
# here is not evidence the locks agree.
#
# Bound to every stamp rather than listed per lane, so a lane added later has
# the edge. `formal-all.stamp` has it too, harmlessly.
extend every stamp
  reads vendor/deps.lock
